1	\section{Schema Decomposition}
2	\label{sec:sdgd}
3	
4	\looseness=-1
5	A natural intervention is to partition the schema so confusable types never co-occur. We tested Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition (SDGD), which applies greedy graph coloring on the SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches. While oracle routing recovers accuracy (0.802 vs.\ 0.504 single-pass), all practical routing strategies fall below the single-pass baseline, and NER decomposition amplifies false positives (F1: 0.445$\to$0.251). Even oracle accuracy falls short of per-type baselines ({>}0.85): merge amplification is the structural bottleneck---union merge doubles false positives (precision: 0.56$\to$0.21) while voting merge sacrifices recall. Architectures that avoid explicit merge (e.g., retrieval-based type selection) may circumvent this. Full protocol and analysis are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}.
6

tool_args.new_string:
    \item We identify structural barriers to confusability-aware extraction: schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification (NER, ED) and routing failure (RE), establishing that the decompose-then-merge paradigm faces structural precision--recall tradeoffs in training-free IE (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{Experimental design overview on FewRel. Each point is one batch; color encodes RE accuracy (green=high, red=low). \textbf{Dimension~1} (horizontal): accuracy declines with type count (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}). \textbf{Dimension~2} (vertical): within each count level, higher BCS correlates with lower accuracy (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}). BCS range narrows at high counts, limiting Dimension~2 discriminability.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

